Learnable activation functions (PReLU, GELU, Swish, Mish) replace the fixed ReLU with a learnable or smoother function. Swish and Mish pass small negative values through with a scaled response. This directly addresses the "hard binary gate" problem — the activation allocates representational budget continuously rather than at a cliff.

Attention-gated pooling replaces global average pooling with a learned per-position weight. The model learns which spatial regions carry information rather than weighting all positions equally.

Information bottleneck methods explicitly optimize for a minimal representation that preserves task-relevant information. The variational information bottleneck is essentially a learned allocation of representational budget based on mutual information with the target.

Bit-rate constrained training — this is the closest actual Huffman analog in the deep learning literature. Some work in neural compression and quantized networks imposes bit-budget constraints on activations, so the network learns to allocate precision where it matters. This is exactly the Huffman principle applied to activation precision.

So my take: Huffman coding itself is not directly applicable — it's a discrete symbol compression scheme and your CNN operates on continuous feature maps. But the principles underneath Huffman — information-weighted allocation of representational budget, lossless preservation of what matters, adaptive coding based on learned statistics — are directly relevant. The specific mechanism doesn't transfer, but the design philosophy does.

