reframing changes everything. not asking "can we apply Huffman compression to CNN features." You're asking: what if the objective of the representation is invertibility — a map that lets you reconstruct the original — rather than class discrimination? And Huffman is the exemplar of that: a code whose defining property is that you can walk it backwards to exactly the original.

This connects directly to things that already exist, but not the way the current literature frames them:

The autoencoder lineage is the closest formal instance. An autoencoder's entire objective is decode(encode(x)) ≈ x. The bottleneck layer is a lossy code, but the training signal is reconstruction, not classification. This is your "map backwards to the original" made explicit. The reason it's relevant here: an autoencoder pretrained on contextual bigrams would be forced to retain everything needed to reconstruct the image, including the character identity. It cannot throw away the "which character is this" information because you need it to reconstruct. Contrastive loss can throw it away — it only needs enough to separate 的-bigrams from non-的-bigrams. Reconstruction cannot.

The "information map" framing is essentially rate-distortion theory. A representation is a code; the question is what rate (bits per sample) you spend and what distortion you tolerate. Huffman is the lossless limit (zero distortion, variable rate). An autoencoder with a bottleneck is the lossy version (fixed rate, nonzero distortion). The question — "what information do we need to get back to the image?" — is asking for the minimal sufficient code for reconstruction. That's a well-posed information-theoretic question.

Why this matters for the actual finding: Model B's conv3 filters do encode class-discriminative information — the spatial probe showed 80.8% — but the contrastive objective didn't ask them to preserve it in a form the architecture could pass through. A reconstruction objective would change what "similar" means during training. Instead of "are these two bigrams the same category," it becomes "can I rebuild this image from this code." The filters would be organized around reconstruction sufficiency, which necessarily includes stroke structure, spatial layout, and character identity — because you can't reconstruct 的 without encoding what 的 looks like.

The "breadcrumbs" metaphor has a precise technical name: the reconstruction path is the information you'd need to invert the forward pass. In an invertible network (Normalizing Flows, RevNets), every layer is exactly invertible — the "map backwards" is architectural, not learned. In a standard autoencoder, the decoder is the learned inverse. The question of "how do we map back" is the decoder. And the failure diagnosic — Model B losing character identity — would be impossible under a reconstruction objective, because a representation that can't rebuild 的 can't rebuild 的.

So the concrete proposal is: pretrain Model B with a reconstruction objective instead of (or in addition to) contrastive loss, and see whether the conv3 representations then survive the ReLU and pooling. The prediction is yes — because reconstruction forces the model to preserve whatever information is needed to rebuild the input, and character identity is part of that. If that holds, we've shown that the original gap was driven by the objective function's choice of what to preserve, not by the pretraining data or the architecture's inherent capacity. That's a cleaner confounder elimination than "replace ReLU with Swish," because it changes what the model is asked to do, not just how it gates.

 a reconstruction objective doesn't guarantee character-discriminative representations any more than contrastive guarantees character-identity representations. A model that reconstructs perfectly could still encode character identity in a nonlinear, entangled form that a linear probe can't read. But that's a different glitch, and it's testable — one probes the reconstruction-trained model's features with linear and nonlinear probes and see which can read character identity. If the information is there but nonlinear, that's a fundamentally different diagnosis than "the information was destroyed."
