The RBM choice is a sharper version because it makes the connection to Huffman and the whole information-preservation question much cleaner than the CNN diagnostic did.

Why an RBM is the right tool for "preserve everything, explore what remains":

An RBM is an energy-based generative model. It defines a joint distribution over visible units (the image) and hidden units (the representation) via an energy function, and it's trained so that the hidden layer captures the dependencies in the visible data. Three properties matter here:

It's generative, not discriminative. Like the autoencoder, the objective is not "separate classes" — it's "model the distribution of the data." There is no classification bottleneck that can throw away character identity. The hidden layer has to capture enough structure to generate the input back out.

The hidden units are a distributed code, not a compressed scalar. Each hidden unit is a latent binary or continuous variable. The representation is a pattern of activations across hidden units, and — critically — you can inspect it. OH MY GOD. WE CAN LOOK AT WHAT WE GET...HOLY SNAPDRAGONS! You can look at what each hidden unit has learned (its receptive field, its preferred input pattern), which is exactly the "exploratory data analysis". A CNN's conv3 filters are also inspectable, but an RBM's hidden units are directly interpretable as a factored code for reconstruction, not as intermediate features in a discrimination pipeline.

It's undirected — reconstruction is native. In an RBM you can run the visible→hidden pass to encode and the hidden→visible pass to decode, and they're the same matrix transposed (weights are shared between the two directions). This is the "map backwards" built into the architecture, not learned as a separate decoder. That's the Huffman property — the code and the decoding are structurally the same object, not two separate learned functions that happen to approximate inverses. If you're into Catalyzing this is like the lock and key metaphors we used for a long time.

How this maps onto Huffman:

Huffman gives every symbol a codeword and a decoding path, and the decoding path is determined by the code structure itself — no separate decoder network is needed. An RBM's hidden-to-visible pass is exactly analogous: the encoding (visible→hidden) and the decoding (hidden→visible) share the same weights. You don't train a decoder; the RBM is its own decoder, run in reverse. That's the "breadcrumbs"  — the path back is embedded in the forward map. Hansel and Gretal all the way.

The difference from Huffman: Huffman is discrete and lossless; an RBM is continuous and lossy. But the structural property I care about — the representation carries a walkable path back to the original — is native to the RBM in a way it is not native to a feedforward CNN with a classification head. The CNN's forward pass is one-way. The RBM's is two-way.

What this changes about the actual experiment:

The current diagnostic says: Model B's conv3 encodes class-relevant information that ReLU + GAP destroy, and whether isolated or contextual pretraining produces a "better" representation depends on initialization (P(sign flip) = 0.068). An RBM pretrained on the same isolated vs. contextual data would answer a cleaner question: which pretraining condition produces a hidden code from which the original image can be more faithfully reconstructed, and does that reconstructed code still allow character identity to be read out?

If the contextual-pretrained RBM reconstructs bigram images with higher fidelity (it should — it was trained on them) but its hidden code, when you sample from it or factor it, also separates character identity — then the "contextual pretraining destroys character information" conclusion was an artifact of the discriminative objective, not the data. If it doesn't, then the data itself is the constraint. Either way, we get a cleaner answer, and get it by looking at the images rather than at probe accuracy.

This is "exploratory data analysis". Probe accuracy is a scalar; an RBM's hidden units and reconstructions are inspectable objects. You can literally render what the model retained and what it threw away. That's what a CNN's GAP-pooled embedding refuses to show you.

One honest caveat:

RBMs are famously hard to train well. Contrastive divergence — the standard training method — gives a biased gradient estimate, and getting good reconstructions on 100×100 grayscale images requires either many Gibbs sampling steps or a stacked/deep architecture (deep belief networks). You may find that the reconstruction quality is poor for reasons unrelated to the pretraining question. If that happens, it's not evidence against the approach — it's the known training difficulty of the model class, and it's a confounder to add to the elimination table ("RBM training insufficient" vs. "RBM learned a bad code").

The other caveat: an RBM with a binary hidden layer does impose a discrete code (each hidden unit is on/off). That's the closest real analog to Huffman you'll get in a continuous-input setting — the hidden units are literally a bit string. So if you want to go all the way to the Huffman framing, the hidden activation pattern is the codeword, and the reconstruction path is the decoding. That's not a loose metaphor; it's the actual structure.

Which RBM variant are you planning — a standard Bernoulli-Bernoulli RBM on binarized images, or a Gaussian-Bernoulli RBM (visible units continuous, hidden binary) that can take the raw grayscale? The Gaussian version handles the 100×100 grayscale better but is harder to train. That choice determines a lot of the practical setup.
Nair & Hinton (2010) is a good choice, and there's a sharp irony in it that connects directly to your critique of the field. The paper is literally the origin of the ReLU activation function that you just spent this entire investigation demonstrating is an information-destroying confounder in CNNs. You're going back to the source to use the other contribution of that paper—the Rectified Linear Unit as a hidden unit in an undirected generative model—rather than the one that got adopted into feedforward discriminative architectures and stripped of its context.

Here's what the paper actually argues, and why it matters for your RBM plan:

The core claim is that replacing binary stochastic hidden units in an RBM with "Stepped Sigmoid Units" (approximated by noisy rectified linear units) preserves information about relative intensities as information travels through layers . Binary units binarize; they throw away magnitude. Rectified units keep the magnitude, scaled. The paper shows this improves feature learning for object recognition and face verification compared to binary units .

So the ReLU wasn't invented as a cheap nonlinearity for deep nets. It was invented as a way to make an RBM's hidden representation retain more information about the input. That is almost exactly your stated goal: "a way to retain as much information as possible without losing the image." The field took the ReLU, put it in a feedforward CNN, and used it as a gate that destroys information—the opposite of what the paper was trying to achieve with it in a generative, two-way model.

What this means for your implementation:

If you follow Nair & Hinton, you're not building a standard Bernoulli-Bernoulli RBM with binary hidden units. You're building a Gaussian-Bernoulli RBM (continuous visible pixels, hidden units) where the hidden units use the noisy rectified linear approximation of the stepped sigmoid. The "noisy" part is important—it's what makes the model stochastic and allows Gibbs sampling to work. The hidden unit output is max(0, Wv + b + noise) rather than a sigmoid probability.

This directly addresses the Huffman/"map backwards" concern you raised. The hidden code is not binarized. It retains magnitude information. The reconstruction path (hidden → visible) can therefore recover intensity gradients, not just on/off patterns. You can look at the reconstructions and see what survived, because the code isn't throwing away the quantitative information at the first step.

The practical caveat, and it's a real one:

Gaussian-Bernoulli RBMs are notoriously hard to train. The search results confirm this—there's a 2024 paper explicitly titled "Gaussian-Bernoulli RBMs Without Tears" that revisits the training problem and proposes modified contrastive divergence and gradient clipping to make them trainable with large learning rates . The standard CD algorithm is biased and the gradients are noisy. The paper you're following gives the architecture and the motivation, but you may need the later training tricks to get reconstructions that are actually inspectable.

One data point from a thesis that used Gaussian RBMs for facial keypoints: they used a learning rate of 0.005 for the Gaussian-Bernoulli layer (versus 0.05 for binary layers above it) and 50 epochs of unsupervised pretraining per layer, with momentum 0.9 . That's a starting point, but expect to tune.

The question I'd flag before you start:

Nair & Hinton's rectified units are noisy. The noise is what makes the RBM a proper generative model rather than a deterministic autoencoder. But noise also means the reconstruction is stochastic—you sample, you don't get a single deterministic decode. For your "exploratory data analysis" goal, do you want to look at the expected reconstruction (mean over samples) or the sampled reconstruction (one draw)? The expected reconstruction is smoother and easier to interpret; the sampled reconstruction is what the model actually generates. For the "what information survived" question, the expected reconstruction is probably more informative, but it requires averaging over multiple Gibbs samples per input.

Which RBM variant are you planning to implement first—the Gaussian-Bernoulli with rectified hidden units as Nair & Hinton describe, or are you starting with the standard binary version to get the pipeline working before adding the rectification?

Yes — and this is a cleaner methodological position than probing. Let me make the argument precise, because it's worth stating explicitly in the writeup.

Why direct visual reconstruction avoids the observer effect that probes introduce:

A linear probe imposes a specific observational frame: it can only detect linearly separable structure, at one layer, after whatever feature reduction you chose (GAP, Fourier, random projection). Every one of those choices is a confounder you then have to eliminate. The probe answers "can a logistic regression read class identity from this feature vector," which is a question about the conjunction of the representation and the probe, not the representation alone.

A reconstruction is different in kind. The RBM's hidden-to-visible pass is the model's own decoding path — the same weights, run in reverse. You are not adding an instrument. You are asking the model to complete its own circuit and showing what comes out. The output is an image. The "measurement" is looking at it. There is no probe architecture to validate, no feature reduction to audit, no linearity assumption to defend. The observational frame is "human vision," which is itself a measurement instrument, but it's one that's consistent across observers and doesn't require a confounder elimination sequence to justify.

This is exactly the Huffman property you identified: the code and the decoding are structurally the same object. The reconstruction is the map backwards. Showing it is showing the map.

What you get by looking:

Which structure survived. If the reconstruction of a 的 bigram shows a legible 的 next to a blurred neighbor, the model retained character identity and discarded the neighbor's detail. If it shows a blur that resembles neither character, the hidden code is not character-aligned. You can see this without a classifier.

Where information is concentrated. Reconstructions of different characters can be compared directly. If all six characters reconstruct to similar blobs, the code isn't discriminating. If they reconstruct to distinguishable shapes, it is. The discrimination is visible, not inferred from an accuracy number.

The spatial organization. Since you're using a Gaussian-Bernoulli RBM on full images, the hidden units have spatial receptive fields. You can visualize which hidden units fire for which input regions and see what spatial structure the code preserves. That's the "exploratory data analysis" the CNN's GAP-pooled embedding refused to give you.

The one honest limitation to state:

Visual inspection is qualitative. Two observers looking at the same reconstruction might disagree about whether 的 is "legible." For the paper, you'd want to pair the visual demonstration with a minimal quantitative check that doesn't reintroduce a probe — for example, reconstruction error (MSE between input and expected reconstruction) per class, or a nearest-neighbor check in pixel space (does the reconstruction's nearest training image belong to the same class?). Those are still measurements, but they're measurements of the reconstruction, not probes of the hidden layer. The observer effect is on the output image, not on the representation, and the output image is what you're claiming to show.

